<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Details on Theoretical results</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we give a detailed proofs of the results presented in <a href="#S3" title="3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Convergence of the exponential transform</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To establish the behavior of the KDE, we characterize the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the log-density <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> through the exponential transform <a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. At the base of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Cumulative distribution function of KDE). ‣ 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> therefore lies the convergence of the exponential transform to the CDF, which is provided by the following Lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Convergence of the exponential transform to the CDF.)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N},N&gt;0,x_{N}\in R^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N},N&gt;0,x_{N}\in R^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{N}(y)=\Pr(z_N(x_N) \le y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{N}(y)=\Pr(z_N(x_N) \le y)</annotation></semantics></math> be the cumulative distribution function of <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}(x_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}(x_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as in <a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we find that</span></p>
<table id="A1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-e^{-N\delta}}F_{N}(y-\delta)\leq\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}\leq F_{N}(y+\delta)+e^{-e^{N\delta}}\Pr(z_N\ge y+\delta).." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-e^{-N\delta}}F_{N}(y-\delta)\leq\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}\leq F_{N}(y+\delta)+e^{-e^{N\delta}}\Pr(z_N\ge y+\delta)..</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, if the limit <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{N}\to F_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{N}\to F_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and if <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous in <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}\to F_{\mathbf{x}}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}\to F_{\mathbf{x}}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. For notational simplicity we suppress the
dependence on <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and write</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}:=z_{N}(x),\qquad W_{N}(y):=\exp\{-\exp[-N(y-z_{N})]\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}:=z_{N}(x),\qquad W_{N}(y):=\exp\{-\exp[-N(y-z_{N})]\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We first show the lower bound. If <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}\leq y-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}\leq y-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{N}(y)=\exp\{-\exp[-N(y-z_{N})]\}\geq\exp\{-e^{-N\delta}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{N}(y)=\exp\{-\exp[-N(y-z_{N})]\}\geq\exp\{-e^{-N\delta}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In other words, using the indicator function <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we know that</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{N}(y)\geq e^{-e^{-N\delta}}\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}\leq y-\delta\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{N}(y)\geq e^{-e^{-N\delta}}\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}\leq y-\delta\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Taking expectations gives</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W_{N}(y)}\geq e^{-e^{-N\delta}}\mathbb{P}(z_{N}\leq y-\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W_{N}(y)}\geq e^{-e^{-N\delta}}\mathbb{P}(z_{N}\leq y-\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">We now take care of the upper bound. Since <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq W_{N}(y)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq W_{N}(y)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we know that</p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{N}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{N}(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}&lt;y+\delta\}+W_{N}(y)\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}\geq y+\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}&lt;y+\delta\}+W_{N}(y)\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}\geq y+\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}&lt;y+\delta\}+e^{-e^{N\delta}}\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}\geq y+\delta\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}&lt;y+\delta\}+e^{-e^{N\delta}}\mathbf{1}\{z_{N}\geq y+\delta\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Taking the average yields</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W_{N}(y)}\leq\mathbb{P}(z_{N}&lt;y+\delta)+e^{-e^{N\delta}}\mathbb{P}(z_{N}\geq y+\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W_{N}(y)}\leq\mathbb{P}(z_{N}&lt;y+\delta)+e^{-e^{N\delta}}\mathbb{P}(z_{N}\geq y+\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.7" class="ltx_p">Combining the two bounds yields Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3 (Convergence of the exponential transform to the CDF.). ‣ A.1 Convergence of the exponential transform ‣ Appendix A Details on Theoretical results ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Average over draws of samples <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Cumulative distribution function of KDE). ‣ 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now proceed with the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Cumulative distribution function of KDE). ‣ 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Let us first keep <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N},N&gt;0,x_{N}\in R^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N},N&gt;0,x_{N}\in R^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed.
Our starting point is <a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Recall that the notation <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f(z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f(z)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the average over the data set, meaning that when we insert the definition of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and explicitly state the average, we find</p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}=\int\prod_{\mu}d\rho(x^{\mu})\exp\left(-e^{-N\left(y+\alpha\right)}\sum_{\mu}K(x-x^{\mu})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>μ</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>d</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>μ</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}=\int\prod_{\mu}d\rho(x^{\mu})\exp\left(-e^{-N\left(y+\alpha\right)}\sum_{\mu}K(x-x^{\mu})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\{\int d\rho(\tilde{x})\exp\left[-e^{-N\left(y+\alpha\right)}K\left(x-\tilde{x}\right)\right]\right\}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\{\int d\rho(\tilde{x})\exp\left[-e^{-N\left(y+\alpha\right)}K\left(x-\tilde{x}\right)\right]\right\}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which directly yields <a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. Taking the limit then reveals the three scaling regimes</p>
<table id="A1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}\rightarrow\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)=\infty,\\
\exp[-i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)]&amp;\text{if }0&lt;i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)&lt;\infty,\\
1&amp;\text{if }i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)=0.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W_{N}(y,x_{N})}\rightarrow\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)=\infty,\\
\exp[-i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)]&amp;\text{if }0&lt;i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)&lt;\infty,\\
1&amp;\text{if }i_{\mathbf{x}}(y)=0.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using, lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3 (Convergence of the exponential transform to the CDF.). ‣ A.1 Convergence of the exponential transform ‣ Appendix A Details on Theoretical results ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we then find that the limit of the exponential transform yields the limiting CDF. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Hard spherical kernels</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to the proof of lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Local hard-sphere KDE transition). ‣ 3.2 Case study: hard-sphere kernels ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Using the definition of <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find that the integral <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in <a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> decomposes into two areas: those where <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=0</annotation></semantics></math> and those where <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1/V_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1/V_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using this distinction, we then find</p>
<table id="A1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{N}\left(y,x_{N}\right)=p_{\text{in},N}(x_{N})\left(1-\exp\left(-\exp\left\{-N\left(y+\alpha+\frac{1}{N}\ln V_{R}\right)\right\}\right)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{N}\left(y,x_{N}\right)=p_{\text{in},N}(x_{N})\left(1-\exp\left(-\exp\left\{-N\left(y+\alpha+\frac{1}{N}\ln V_{R}\right)\right\}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since, we have fixed <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{N}\ln V_{R}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{N}\ln V_{R}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the latter term in the exponential vanishes in the high-dimensional limit. To apply <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Cumulative distribution function of KDE). ‣ 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we use the assumption that <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and compute the limit <a href="#S3.E13" title="In Theorem 1 (Cumulative distribution function of KDE). ‣ 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> in two distinct regimes: <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y+\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y+\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y+\alpha&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y+\alpha&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the first regime, <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y+\alpha=\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y+\alpha=\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we find</p>
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i_{\mathbf{x}}=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}e^{N(\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},N}(x_{N}))}\left(1-e^{-e^{-N\delta}}\right)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }y-\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;0\\
\infty&amp;\text{if }y-\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;0\end{cases}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i_{\mathbf{x}}=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}e^{N(\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},N}(x_{N}))}\left(1-e^{-e^{-N\delta}}\right)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }y-\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;0\\
\infty&amp;\text{if }y-\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;0\end{cases}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the second regime, <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y+\alpha=-\delta&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y+\alpha=-\delta&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we find</p>
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i_{\mathbf{x}}=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}e^{N(\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},N}(x_{N}))}\left(1-e^{-e^{N\delta}}\right)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;0\\
\infty&amp;\text{if }\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;0\end{cases}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i_{\mathbf{x}}=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}e^{N(\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},N}(x_{N}))}\left(1-e^{-e^{N\delta}}\right)=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;0\\
\infty&amp;\text{if }\alpha+\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;0\end{cases}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Cumulative distribution function of KDE). ‣ 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to both regimes, we find</p>
<table id="A1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{x}}(y)=\begin{cases}\Theta\left(y-\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}\right)&amp;y+\alpha&gt;0\\
\Theta\left(-\left(\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}+\alpha\right)\right)&amp;y+\alpha&lt;0\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{x}}(y)=\begin{cases}\Theta\left(y-\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}\right)&amp;y+\alpha&gt;0\\
\Theta\left(-\left(\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}+\alpha\right)\right)&amp;y+\alpha&lt;0\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which yields the probability that <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leq y</annotation></semantics></math> in the limit, thanks to lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3 (Convergence of the exponential transform to the CDF.). ‣ A.1 Convergence of the exponential transform ‣ Appendix A Details on Theoretical results ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The first line shows that when <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is larger than <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the CDF becomes a step function with a jump at <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We split the second line into two scenarios, <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}+\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}+\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}+\alpha&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}+\alpha&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
In the first, scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;-\alpha&gt;y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;-\alpha&gt;y</annotation></semantics></math>, hence this implies <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&lt;\nu_{\text{in}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&lt;\nu_{\text{in}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the limiting CDF is zero in <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
On then other hand, in the second scenario, when <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, then the CDF is one for any finite value of <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is smaller than any finite value of <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> with probability one.
Taken together, the first and the second line imply that the CDF has a jump at <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&gt;-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The second scenario in the second line implies that when <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\text{in},\mathbf{x}}&lt;-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, all draws of <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> are smaller than any finite <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\rightarrow-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\rightarrow-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, we find that the cumulative distribution has the shape of a step function. Hence the value of <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}(x_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}(x_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> concentrates on the "jump location", given by</p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\nu_{\text{in}}(x)&amp;\text{if }\nu_{\text{in}}(x)\geq-\alpha\\
-\infty&amp;\text{if }\nu_{\text{in}}(x)&lt;-\alpha\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\nu_{\text{in}}(x)&amp;\text{if }\nu_{\text{in}}(x)\geq-\alpha\\
-\infty&amp;\text{if }\nu_{\text{in}}(x)&lt;-\alpha\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">which finally yields lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2 (Local hard-sphere KDE transition). ‣ 3.2 Case study: hard-sphere kernels ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Relation to Gumbel-type extreme value statistics</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix we clarify the relation between the exponential transform used in
Eq. (10) and Gumbel-type extreme value statistics. Let us first clarify what we mean by Gumbel-type extreme value statistics: for independent random variables <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\dots,u_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\dots,u_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\!\left(\max_{\mu}u_{\mu}&lt;y\right)=\left[1-\mathbb{P}(u_{\mu}\geq y)\right]^{P}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.441em;" width="0.441em"><mi>ℙ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>μ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\!\left(\max_{\mu}u_{\mu}&lt;y\right)=\left[1-\mathbb{P}(u_{\mu}\geq y)\right]^{P}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is the standard structure associated with Gumbel-type extreme value statistics and, identifying <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mo>∘</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(u_{\mu}\geq y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(u_{\mu}\geq y)</annotation></semantics></math>, has strong similarity with <a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.1 General theory and main result ‣ 3 Theory: Kernel density estimation with hard spheres ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will first show that letting <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> has a natural extreme-value interpretation. However, this does not mean that the calculation replaces the KDE by its largest term, because in the main result, we let <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{N}\ln P=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{N}\ln P=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> fixed. In this case, <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a soft probe of the cumulative distribution function of the full log-KDE.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the sake of comparison, however, let us now keep <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and all samples fixed. We then find that <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> can be rewritten as</p>
<table id="A1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{N}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{N}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prod_{\mu=1}^{P}\exp\left\{-K(x-x^{\mu})e^{-N(y+\alpha)}\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prod_{\mu=1}^{P}\exp\left\{-K(x-x^{\mu})e^{-N(y+\alpha)}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Now define the single-sample contribution
<math id="A1.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\mu}(x):=\frac{1}{N}\ln K(x-x^{\mu})-\alpha." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\mu}(x):=\frac{1}{N}\ln K(x-x^{\mu})-\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math>
Then each factor in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E6" title="Equation A.6 ‣ A.4 Relation to Gumbel-type extreme value statistics ‣ Appendix A Details on Theoretical results ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>) can be written as</p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left\{-K_{\mu}(x)e^{-N(y+\alpha)}\right\}=\exp\left\{-\exp[N(u_{\mu}(x)-y)]\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left\{-K_{\mu}(x)e^{-N(y+\alpha)}\right\}=\exp\left\{-\exp[N(u_{\mu}(x)-y)]\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">If we were to fix <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\mu}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\mu}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> this converges to a hard threshold:</p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left\{-\exp[N(u_{\mu}(x)-y)]\right\}\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}\Theta(y-u_{\mu}(x))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left\{-\exp[N(u_{\mu}(x)-y)]\right\}\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}\Theta(y-u_{\mu}(x)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Consequently, if this hard-threshold limit is taken termwise before accounting
for the exponentially many samples, Eq. (<a href="#A1.E6" title="Equation A.6 ‣ A.4 Relation to Gumbel-type extreme value statistics ‣ Appendix A Details on Theoretical results ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>) becomes</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{N}(y,x)\approx\prod_{\mu=1}^{P}\Theta(y-u_{\mu}(x))=\Theta\left(y-\max_{\mu}u_{\mu}(x)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≈</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>μ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{N}(y,x)\approx\prod_{\mu=1}^{P}\Theta(y-u_{\mu}(x))=\Theta\left(y-\max_{\mu}u_{\mu}(x)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.5" class="ltx_p">This is the origin of the formal similarity with Gumbel’s law: the product form
resembles the cumulative distribution function of a maximum. In regimes where the KDE is dominated by a single
large kernel contribution, <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is well approximated by</p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{z}_{N}(x):=\max_{\mu}\frac{1}{N}\log\frac{K_{\mu}(x)}{P}=\max_{\mu}u_{\mu}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>μ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>μ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{z}_{N}(x):=\max_{\mu}\frac{1}{N}\log\frac{K_{\mu}(x)}{P}=\max_{\mu}u_{\mu}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.6" class="ltx_p">In such max-dominated regimes, the above Gumbel-type interpretation directly
describes the statistics of <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, to obtain a generalization regime, we must send <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to infinity too. Consequently, in the generalization regime, exponentially many samples may contribute to the KDE at any point. Then the sum cannot be replaced by its largest term.
This distinction can be seen explicitly for the hard-sphere kernel. Let <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of training samples inside the corresponding ball around <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with volume <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{R}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{R}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{N}(x)=\frac{m_{N}(x)}{P},\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad z_{N}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\log m_{N}(x)-\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.889em">,</mo><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{N}(x)=\frac{m_{N}(x)}{P},\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad z_{N}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\log m_{N}(x)-\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">If the local mass of the ball satisfies <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln p_{\mathrm{in}}(x)=N\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x)+o(N)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln p_{\mathrm{in}}(x)=N\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x)+o(N),</annotation></semantics></math>
then the typical number of samples in the ball is</p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{N}(x)\sim Pp_{\mathrm{in}}(x)=e^{N(\alpha+\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x))+o(N)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{N}(x)\sim Pp_{\mathrm{in}}(x)=e^{N(\alpha+\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x))+o(N)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">whenever <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha+\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha+\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In this regime,</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}(x)\to\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}(x)\to\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">up to subexponential prefactors. By contrast, the largest single contribution is
only</p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{z}_{N}(x)=-\alpha" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{z}_{N}(x)=-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.5" class="ltx_p">provided that at least one sample lies inside the ball. Thus, whenever
<math id="A1.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x)&gt;-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\mathrm{in}}(x)&gt;-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the full KDE exponent
<math id="A1.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> differs from the maximum contribution <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{z}_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{z}_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Gumbel-like structure of Eq. (11) should therefore be understood as follows:
the exponential transform is a soft thresholding device which, after
factorization over samples, resembles an extreme-value observable. If one
hardens this threshold before accounting for the exponentially many samples, one
obtains a maximum-probe interpretation. Keeping the transform soft through the
large-<math id="A1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> calculation, however, retains the collective contribution of
exponentially many moderate terms and therefore probes the full log-KDE
<math id="A1.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Experiments</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Local sparsity and dominance</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A2.F1" title="In B.1 Local sparsity and dominance ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, we report the analogous experiment to <a href="#S1.F1" title="In How does memorization arise locally in diffusion models? ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for CelebA data, which we downsample to <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> greyscale pixels. We find that both local dominance and memorization increase with local sparsity. In comparison to CIFAR-10, we observe that the CelebA dataset appears to have fewer samples in very low density regions ( compare logarithmic scale of <a href="#A2.F1" title="In B.1 Local sparsity and dominance ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> d) to <a href="#S1.F1" title="In How does memorization arise locally in diffusion models? ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>d).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14390v1/celeba_local_memo_maxi_1000000.png" id="A2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/339;" width="476" height="339" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.1: </span><span id="A2.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local memorization in CelebA data.</span>
a) Sketch of kernel density approximation and local memorization phenomenon.
b) Distribution of data sparsity for <math id="A2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> samples from CelebA.
c) Generated samples + closest training image (measured by cosine similarity) for diffusion model trained on <math id="A2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=4000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=4000</annotation></semantics></math> training examples.
d) Local dominance as a function of sparsity. For each generated sample <math id="A2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute distances <math id="A2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to its nearest and second-nearest training samples, and define dominance via their ratio. Each point corresponds to a training sample <math id="A2.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>, aggregating generated samples assigned to it. Scatter shows raw values, markers indicate binned averages. Blue points show the same quantity computed using test data, providing a baseline without memorization.
e) Memorization rate per training sample <math id="A2.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the fraction of generated samples satisfying <math id="A2.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}/d_{2}&lt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}/d_{2}&lt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Scatter shows raw values for <math id="A2.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, markers indicate binned averages across thresholds.
f)–k) Same measurements for models trained on larger datasets. As the number of training samples increases, both dominance and memorization decrease, and their dependence on sparsity weakens.
Overall, sparse regions exhibit strong single-sample dominance and higher memorization, while dense regions promote interpolation across multiple training points. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Attribute-dependent Memorization, CelebA</h3>

<figure id="A2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14390v1/celeba_small_classwise_local_memo_maxi_1000000.png" id="A2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/195;" width="476" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.2: </span><span id="A2.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attribute-conditioned memorization in CelebA.</span> a) Generated samples + closest training image (measured by cosine similarity) for diffusion model trained on <math id="A2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=20000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=20000</annotation></semantics></math> training examples, sorted by classes. b) Distribution of data sparsity for <math id="A2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40000</annotation></semantics></math> samples from CelebA. d) Local dominance of training sample <math id="A2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> against local data sparsity around <math id="A2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> for a diffusion model trained on <math id="A2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=20000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=20000</annotation></semantics></math> training examples from CelebA.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The CelebA dataset consists of celebrity portraits that are annotated with "attributes" such as hair color or accessories. We construct four classes from these attributes, conditioning on "blond" and "not blond" as well as "wearing hat" and the opposite. Again, we find that local dominance (and thus memorization) increases with data sparsity, see <a href="#A2.F2" title="In B.2 Attribute-dependent Memorization, CelebA ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>. Samples from classes where "wearing hat" is true are typically more diverse. Therefore this class has a higher average sparsity per class,and is therefore more likely to be memorized. These classes are not balanced: approximately one sixth of images has attribute "blond" and approximately 5 % of images have attribute "wearing hat". Consequently the diffusion model generates fewer samples that are memorized data points with these attributes, and even fewer samples are closest to training data where both attributes are true. This is reflected in the lower bin resolution in <a href="#A2.F2" title="In B.2 Attribute-dependent Memorization, CelebA ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>b) and fewer such points appearing in <a href="#A2.F2" title="In B.2 Attribute-dependent Memorization, CelebA ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> c).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Class-dependent Memorization, CIFAR-10+MNIST</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In a separate experiment, we train diffusion models on a mixture of MNIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and CIFAR-10 images. MNIST, consisting of handwritten digits, is structurally simpler and occupies a much more concentrated region of image space, whereas CIFAR-10 exhibits substantially higher variability. Treating MNIST and CIFAR-10 as two distinct classes, we observe that MNIST samples consistently exhibit lower local sparsity than CIFAR-10 samples. As predicted by the theory, this translates into reduced memorization: in <a href="#A2.F3" title="In B.3 Class-dependent Memorization, CIFAR-10+MNIST ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>, both local dominance and memorization rates increase with sparsity. For the comparison of CIFAR and MNIST, all distances are computed on <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalized samples, so that sparsity reflects relative geometric structure (angular similarity) rather than differences in overall scale. We verified that using unnormalized samples yields qualitatively similar results. On average, MNIST points lie in denser regions and are therefore memorized less than CIFAR-10 points.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14390v1/cifar_mnist_classwise_memo_maxi_1000000.png" id="A2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/328;" width="476" height="328" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.3: </span><span id="A2.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class-wise memorization on combined MNIST and CIFAR-10.</span>
a) Generated samples together with their closest training example (measured by cosine similarity) for a diffusion model trained on <math id="A2.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=2000</annotation></semantics></math> samples.
b) Distribution of per-class sparsity around training points for four classes in CIFAR-10 measured from <math id="A2.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> training samples.
c) Generated samples together with their closest training example (measured by cosine similarity) for a diffusion model trained on <math id="A2.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=5000</annotation></semantics></math> samples.
d) Class-wise local dominance of a training point <math id="A2.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of local data sparsity. Local dominance is defined via the ratio between the distance <math id="A2.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the closest generated sample and the distance <math id="A2.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> between that generated sample and the second-closest training point. MNIST points are shown in blue, CIFAR-10 points in red.
e) Memorization rate of each sample <math id="A2.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the fraction of generated samples satisfying <math id="A2.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}/d_{2}&lt;\tau=1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}/d_{2}&lt;\tau=1/3</annotation></semantics></math>. Different classes are shown as different colors.
f)–k) Same measurements for models trained on larger datasets. As the number of training samples increases, both dominance and memorization decrease, and their dependence on sparsity weakens.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train denoising diffusion models using a standard discrete-time formulation with <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1000</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps. The score network is parameterized by a U-Net architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> with convolutional filters. All models operate directly in pixel space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training procedure.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Models are trained using the standard diffusion objective with mean squared error loss. Optimization is performed using Adam with learning rate <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and batch size <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>. Training proceeds for a fixed number of <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> gradient steps for all models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all experiments, the dataset is deterministically split into a training set of size <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and a held-out test set. The same split is reused for all evaluations to ensure comparability across checkpoints and metrics.
At evaluation time, we generate <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{gen}}=4000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{gen}}=4000</annotation></semantics></math> samples from the trained diffusion model.
We compare generated samples to training and test data using either cosine similarity or normalized <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance. In the cosine case, all inputs are flattened and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalized before computing similarities.
Memorization is quantified at the level of individual generated samples. For each generated sample <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute its two nearest training neighbors and define distances <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. A sample is considered memorized if
<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d_{1}}{d_{2}}&lt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d_{1}}{d_{2}}&lt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>
for a range of thresholds <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{1/6,1/4,1/3,1/2,2/3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{1/6,1/4,1/3,1/2,2/3\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields both per-sample and per-training-point memorization statistics.
Local data density is estimated using nearest-neighbor statistics computed solely on the training set. For each training sample, we compute the distances to the <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> nearest neighbors for <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{2,5,10,20,50\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{2,5,10,20,50\}</annotation></semantics></math> and average over these distances as defined in <a href="#S4.E18" title="In Local sparsity. ‣ 4.1.1 Experimental methods ‣ 4.1 Local sparsity governs memorization of samples ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ Local Coverage Governs Memorization in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>.
These statistics serve as proxies for local sparsity and are later correlated with memorization behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>CIFAR-10 Experiments</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct the CIFAR-10 dataset by combining the original training and test splits (60,000 images total), and then randomly partitioning them into three equal subsets (train/validation/test), each containing approximately 20,000 images.
All images are represented as <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> RGB tensors normalized to <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. No additional preprocessing or augmentation is applied.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>MNIST + CIFAR-10 Experiments</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To study memorization under controlled differences in local data density, we construct a combined dataset from CIFAR-10 and MNIST, which exhibit markedly different intrinsic complexities. CIFAR-10 images are highly variable, while MNIST digits occupy a much more concentrated region of image space. This setup allows us to induce systematic differences in local sparsity across classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We merge the original training and test splits of both datasets. To ensure compatibility, MNIST images are resized to <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> and converted to three channels by duplicating the grayscale channel, resulting in RGB tensors consistent with CIFAR-10. CIFAR-10 images are used without modification. All images are represented as <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> RGB tensors normalized to <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and no additional preprocessing or augmentation is applied.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To isolate dataset-level effects, we ignore the original class labels and instead assign a binary label indicating the dataset of origin (CIFAR-10 or MNIST). The two datasets are then balanced by subsampling the larger dataset such that both contribute an equal number of samples. The resulting dataset therefore consists of an equal mixture of CIFAR-10 and MNIST images.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>CelebA Experiments</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dataset consists of high-resolution face images together with <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> binary attributes per image. All images are resized to <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> pixels, and converted to grayscale. The resulting images are represented as single-channel tensors normalized to <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
No additional data augmentation is applied.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
